---
import {
  AiChat02Icon,
  Database01Icon,
  Key01Icon,
  SearchList01Icon,
  UserGroupIcon,
} from '@hugeicons/core-free-icons'
import CodeBlock from '@/components/CodeBlock.astro'
import FeaturePage from '@/components/FeaturePage.astro'

const related = [
  { title: 'seo research', text: 'Every research action, its inputs and how to run it from the CLI.', href: '/docs/research', icon: SearchList01Icon },
  { title: 'API keys', text: 'Add a DataForSEO or Ahrefs key to the hosted MCP or the CLI.', href: '/docs/keys', icon: Key01Icon },
  { title: 'SEO report', text: 'Quick wins, striking distance and AI-style queries from your own Search Console.', href: '/docs/report', icon: Database01Icon },
  { title: 'Competitor keyword research', text: 'See what any domain ranks for in your browser, with no account.', href: '/tools/competitor-keyword-research', icon: UserGroupIcon, link: 'Free tool' },
  { title: 'AI visibility checker', text: 'Whether ChatGPT search, AI Mode and AI Overviews cite your site.', href: '/features/ai-visibility-checker', icon: AiChat02Icon, link: 'AI visibility checker' },
  { title: 'SEO competitor analysis', text: 'Find the sites that compete with you in Google and the pages that earn their traffic.', href: '/features/seo-competitor-analysis', icon: UserGroupIcon, link: 'Competitor analysis' },
  { title: 'Keyword gap analysis', text: 'The searches your competitors rank for and you don\'t, set beside your own queries.', href: '/features/keyword-gap', icon: SearchList01Icon, link: 'Keyword gap' },
]
---

<FeaturePage
  title="AI keyword research tool"
  metaTitle="AI keyword research tool and MCP for agents"
  description="Keyword research in Claude, Codex or ChatGPT through an MCP server: real search volumes, SERPs and competitor keywords from your DataForSEO or Ahrefs key."
  path="/features/keyword-research"
  lead="Ask your agent for keyword ideas, search volume, who ranks and what a competitor ranks for. It answers from your Search Console and your own DataForSEO or Ahrefs account."
  {related}
  relatedHeading="Read more"
>
  <h2>Why an agent needs real keyword data</h2>
  <p>
    Ask a chat model for the search volume of a keyword and it will give you
    a number. That number is a guess. Models have no live keyword database,
    so the volumes, difficulty scores and "top ranking pages" they list are
    made up to look plausible.
  </p>
  <p>
    SEO Skill gives your agent the real thing through an MCP server: your
    own Search Console queries, plus keyword, SERP, competitor and backlink
    data from your DataForSEO or Ahrefs account. The agent still does the
    thinking (grouping keywords, spotting gaps, picking a target), but every
    number it quotes came from a source you can check.
  </p>

  <h2>Set up keyword research in your agent</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Add the hosted MCP server to Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, ChatGPT or any
      client with remote MCP, and sign in with Google. The commands are at
      the top of this page.
    </li>
    <li>
      Save a <a href="/docs/keys">DataForSEO or Ahrefs key</a> on your
      account page. The provider bills you directly, and SEO Skill adds
      nothing on top. Every DataForSEO result says what the request cost.
    </li>
    <li>Ask for keyword research in plain words, like the prompts below.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Prefer a terminal? <code>npm i -g seo</code> then <code>seo start</code>{' '}
    gives you the same research as the <a href="/docs/research">seo research</a>{' '}
    command, with the key kept on your machine.
  </p>

  <h2>Start from the searches you already get</h2>
  <p>
    For a site that already gets traffic, start with the searches Google
    already shows it for. Before you spend a credit, ask for the queries
    where you are close:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock value={`Show me the striking-distance and quick-wins queries for example.com`} wrap />
  <p>
    The <a href="/docs/report">SEO report</a> lists{' '}
    <a href="/features/striking-distance-keywords">striking distance keywords</a>{' '}
    just outside the top positions and pages that rank but get fewer clicks
    than they should. The <code>ai-queries</code> section finds searches that read like
    AI prompts and follow-ups, which often name a gap in your content. This
    is Search Console data, so it is what people actually typed.
  </p>
  <p>
    For every query with its page, clicks and position, ask for an{' '}
    <a href="/features/keyword-report">SEO keyword report</a>. To pick which
    searches to work on first, the{' '}
    <a href="/features/keyword-opportunities">keyword opportunities</a> page
    shows how each one is scored by the clicks it could win.
  </p>

  <h2>Find long-tail keywords from a seed</h2>
  <CodeBlock value={`Give me keyword ideas for "invoice template" and "invoice generator" in the UK`} wrap />
  <p>
    Keyword ideas take up to 5 seed keywords and return the searches that
    contain each one, highest volume first. That is where the long tail
    lives: the "free invoice template for contractors" searches that one
    focused page can win when the head term cannot.
  </p>

  <h2>Size keywords with volume, difficulty and CPC</h2>
  <CodeBlock value={`Get search volume, keyword difficulty and CPC for this list of 40 keywords`} wrap />
  <p>
    Keyword metrics take up to 100 keywords at once. Read cost per click
    next to volume: a keyword advertisers pay $20 a click for usually has
    buyers behind it, even at 50 searches a month.
  </p>

  <h2>Check who ranks before you pick a keyword</h2>
  <CodeBlock value={`Show the top 10 for "ai tools for keyword research" with each site's Domain Rating`} wrap />
  <p>
    Difficulty scores are a provider's model. The results page is the
    evidence. A SERP request lists the ranking pages for up to 5 keywords,
    each with its domain's authority and a free column of Domain Rating by{' '}
    <a href="https://ahrefs.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Ahrefs</a>,
    and the sites an AI Overview cites when Google shows one. A page one full of
    sites weaker than yours is a better bet than a lower difficulty score.
  </p>
  <p>
    To decide whether a set of keywords needs one page or several, ask for
    a SERP overlap. It counts how many top 10 pages each pair of searches
    shares. Lots of shared pages usually means Google treats them as one
    topic.
  </p>

  <h2>Find the keywords your competitors rank for</h2>
  <CodeBlock value={`What does competitor.example rank for that example.com doesn't?`} wrap />
  <p>
    Ranked keywords list what a site, a subfolder such as{' '}
    <code>competitor.example/blog/</code>, or one page ranks for. Top pages
    show where a competitor's search traffic comes from, and competitors
    finds the domains that share your keywords. Your agent can set those
    beside your own Search Console queries to find the gaps. The two
    sources stay apart: a provider's estimate never gets added to your
    clicks. The <a href="/features/keyword-gap">keyword gap analysis</a>{' '}
    page walks through that comparison, and{' '}
    <a href="/features/seo-competitor-analysis">SEO competitor analysis</a>{' '}
    starts one step earlier, with who your search competitors are.
  </p>

  <h2>See whether AI answers cite you</h2>
  <p>
    Keyword research for AI search starts from the questions people ask.
    With a DataForSEO key, your agent can ask ChatGPT search, Google AI Mode
    and AI Overviews up to 10 questions and see whether each answer cites or
    mentions your site, and which sites it cites instead. A run of 10
    questions costs about 7 cents. The <a href="/features/ai-visibility-checker">AI
    visibility checker</a> page covers it in full.
  </p>

  <h2>A real example from this page</h2>
  <p>
    We researched this page the way it describes. Keyword ideas for "ai
    keyword research" and "keyword research mcp", then keyword metrics, from
    DataForSEO for the US in November 2026. Searches are monthly, and KD is
    DataForSEO's keyword difficulty from 0 to 100:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Keyword</th><th>Searches (KD)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>keyword research</td><td>135,000 (KD 88)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>keyword research tool</td><td>3,600 (KD 81)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ai keyword research tool</td><td>170 (KD 68)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>long tail keyword research tool</td><td>140 (KD 32)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ai tools for keyword research</td><td>110 (KD 32)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>how to use ai for keyword research</td><td>50 (KD 5)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>keyword research mcp</td><td>10 (no KD)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The head terms belong to the big keyword tools, and a new page will not
    move them. The SERP for "ai tools for keyword research" told a
    different story: four of its top six results came from sites with a
    Domain Rating under 20. That is the kind of keyword a focused page can win, so it is one
    this page aims at.
  </p>

  <h2>What each research action returns</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Ask for</th><th>What comes back</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Keyword ideas</td><td>Searches containing up to 5 seed keywords, by volume.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Keyword metrics</td><td>Search volume, keyword difficulty and cost per click for up to 100 keywords.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SERP</td><td>The pages that rank for up to 5 keywords, with authority and AI Overview sources.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SERP overlap</td><td>How many top 10 pages 2 to 10 keywords share, pair by pair.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ranked keywords</td><td>The keywords a domain, subfolder or page ranks for.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Top pages</td><td>The pages with the most estimated search traffic.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Competitors</td><td>Domains that rank for the same keywords.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>AI answers</td><td>Whether AI answers cite or mention a site (DataForSEO only).</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Every request takes a two-letter country code. Backlinks are covered on
    the <a href="/tools/backlink-checker">backlink checker</a>, link gaps on
    the <a href="/features/backlink-gap">backlink gap analysis</a> page, and
    both in the <a href="/docs/research">seo research</a> docs.
  </p>

  <h2>What keyword data cannot tell you</h2>
  <p>
    Search volumes are estimates. DataForSEO reports Google Ads' 12-month
    average, Ahrefs uses its own model, and the two can differ several
    times over for the same keyword, so compare keywords within one
    provider. A keyword with no data has an unknown volume, not zero.
    Difficulty is each provider's own score. When you want to know what your pages actually get, your
    Search Console is the number to trust.
  </p>
</FeaturePage>
